WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:05.879
It's great to welcome to the program today,
Tom rivet Karnak who is coauthor of the book

00:00:05.879 --> 00:00:06.879
future.

00:00:06.879 --> 00:00:10.099
We choose surviving the climate crisis.

00:00:10.099 --> 00:00:15.679
Tom's also a leader in the fields of international
diplomacy, energy policy and climate change.

00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:18.710
Three areas, Tom, that are relevant.

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:24.140
If you're going to be involved in the negotiation
of the Paris climate Accords, which you you

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:25.420
ended up participating in.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:28.600
How did that even come about?

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:29.600
I did.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.250
Well, it was, it was a great privilege to
be so closely involved in the Paris climate

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:34.250
Accords.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:37.320
I was actually working in New York where I
am right now when I was working at an NGO

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:43.790
called CDP, where I was helping companies
identify the types of strategies that would

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:47.739
make them more effective, um, to deal with
the climate crisis.

00:00:47.739 --> 00:00:51.570
And in that position, uh, through a friend,
I got to meet Christiana Figueres, who's my

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:56.329
co author on this book and at the time was
the executive secretary of the UN climate

00:00:56.329 --> 00:00:57.680
convention.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:03.180
And we spent a day together in New York around,
and what was clear was that she was obviously

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:08.520
surrounded by many brilliant people inside
the UN, but there tends to be the emergence

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:13.869
of not group think might be a little unfair,
but sort of the same solutions to the same

00:01:13.869 --> 00:01:16.749
problems that you've been struggling with
for a long time.

00:01:16.749 --> 00:01:20.950
And she knew that she wanted somebody in there
who could think about the political challenges

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:22.060
in a new way.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:26.460
And so that's why she hired me to come and
run the political strategy a few years before

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:27.490
Paris.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:34.490
So one of the common, I guess, refrains that
I hear from people who take as seriously as

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:40.899
we should be, the, the issue of climate change
about the Paris, uh, um, uh, accord is that

00:01:40.899 --> 00:01:44.689
the final result feels sort of underwhelming.

00:01:44.689 --> 00:01:50.890
In other words, all of this negotiation just
to say, well, we'll commit to limit the increase

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:53.950
of temperature above pre-industrial levels.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:59.609
Can you talk a little bit about what the negotiation
is actually like and, and why?

00:01:59.609 --> 00:02:04.499
Just looking at the outcome ignores a lot
of what goes into even doing this in the first

00:02:04.499 --> 00:02:05.499
place.

00:02:05.499 --> 00:02:06.499
Sure.

00:02:06.499 --> 00:02:07.700
No, I can definitely talk about that.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.580
And, and but one contextualize in comment
because you're absolutely right.

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:15.780
And what you said is, is negotiations and
outcomes are pathways and that only meaningful

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:16.780
if they're followed.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:19.129
That's where, that's interesting.

00:02:19.129 --> 00:02:23.930
So for many years the negotiations had been
mired in this issue of fairness, right?

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:29.519
So developing countries said to develop countries,
you caused this problem.

00:02:29.519 --> 00:02:34.760
And what's more, you said you'd sought it
out in 1991 when the UN first started thinking

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:35.830
about this.

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:37.841
So go away and do something meaningful about
it.

00:02:37.841 --> 00:02:40.950
And then come back and we'll talk about a
global agreement.

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:45.020
Whereas the developed countries would say,
well, the world is different now.

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:49.420
Many of the emissions actually come from developing
countries, India, China, many other places

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:51.470
are developing very rapidly.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:55.629
This now needs to be an everyone everywhere
type of an endeavor.

00:02:55.629 --> 00:02:58.700
And the challenges that both of those perspectives
were correct.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:02:59.700
Right?

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:02.200
And both of them were justified and very defensible.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:05.901
So for a long time, the discussion in the
climate negotiations was about, well, whose

00:03:05.901 --> 00:03:07.860
responsibility is this?

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:12.920
And it became mired in this sort of zero sum
mentality of this was a burden that nobody

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:14.409
wanted to take on.

00:03:14.409 --> 00:03:17.150
And everybody was trying to pass around between
each other.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:21.349
One of the things I think we did in Paris,
or at least we took serious steps towards

00:03:21.349 --> 00:03:26.069
doing, although it's unfinished business,
is we flipped that, you know, dealing with

00:03:26.069 --> 00:03:29.109
the climate crisis is of course an urgent
challenge.

00:03:29.109 --> 00:03:31.780
It is also the biggest economic opportunity.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:34.740
It's the biggest opportunity to improve the
quality of our lives.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:39.430
It's the biggest opportunity to improve our
biodiversity, our, our, our quality of air,

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:41.109
et cetera, et cetera.

00:03:41.109 --> 00:03:45.010
And we started to think about it more around
less as a burden to be shared and more of

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:47.190
an opportunity to be captured.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:53.049
So that changed the nature of the negotiations
to a degree where every country would agree

00:03:53.049 --> 00:03:56.870
collectively we need to solve this in the
long term and for the next few years I'll

00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:00.489
put down my, as to what I'll do for the next
few years.

00:04:00.489 --> 00:04:04.480
That's consistent with my politics that is
of benefit to me as a country.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:09.400
And that enabled the spirit to begin to change,
which was the real breakthrough we needed.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:15.709
So there's a whole range of estimates that
are made about what might the planet look

00:04:15.709 --> 00:04:19.850
like in five 10 30 et cetera, years.

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:25.410
There's not wholesale agreement, although
there are straight out bad faith actors and

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:29.360
then there are just sort of like reasonable
disagreements within the the the communities

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:32.030
that are appropriately dealing with this in
good faith.

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:38.590
In the book you outline, you know, a possible
scenario where we just fail to deal with this

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:43.120
issue and what 2050 might look like in that
scenario.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:49.590
And then you also talk about moving in the
direction by 2050 of carbon neutrality, regenerated

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:54.440
economies and Regina, you know, moving in
the right direction so to speak, is part of

00:04:54.440 --> 00:05:01.600
the problem on this that even for people who
believe the science, it's sort of difficult

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:08.480
to really believe that nightmare scenario
because it seems so horrifying and that may

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:13.140
cause some sort of paralysis and actually
getting going on it.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:15.410
Yeah, I think that's a great point.

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:20.640
And I think that there has been this massive
discrepancy between the world we see around

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.630
us, which until recently has largely carried
on as we've more or less known it.

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:30.610
And these sort of very challenging, very alarming
predictions of periods of time that are just

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:32.030
a few decades in the future.

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:36.630
And I think for many people it can be difficult
to bring those two things together, particularly

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:39.120
when the solutions that are proposed seem
small, right?

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:42.090
They seem like small iterations on your life.

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:48.180
Sadly, it's becoming more and more possible
to imagine what that negative future might

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:49.180
look like.

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:50.180
Right?

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:53.830
I mean just in the last few months, if we
look at the terrible devastation of the wildfires

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:58.950
in Australia and the unbelievable loss of
life and property and the heartbreaking loss

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:03.590
of biodiversity, we look at the, you know,
the disappearance of coral reefs around the

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:04.590
world.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.610
We look at the warming of the oceans and the
hotspots.

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:11.200
We look at the burning of the Amazon and of
Siberia and of California, the floods in England,

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:13.300
the melting of Antarctica.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:18.900
Unfortunately we are getting sharper relief
of what it could be like.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:24.900
And these early signs should wake us up because
science tells us we still have time, right?

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:31.590
This decade I would argue, will end up being
the most consequential decade in the history

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:32.650
of humanity.

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:36.590
Now, that's a lot for us to take on board,
but we have to face that and we have to face

00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:42.010
it now if we're going to have the kind of
impact that we need to have to improve the

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:44.550
world in the way that we still can.

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:49.330
So on the personal versus structural, we've
talked before about how, you know, putting

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:55.950
a disproportionate focus on plastic straws
distorts the reality that a vast majority,

00:06:55.950 --> 00:07:02.160
what we have to deal with is actually the
output in waste from large corporations, particularly

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:06.410
in certain industries that are the, the, the
disproportionate contributors.

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:12.020
So if you talk about plastic straws and making
sure that you're sorting your cardboard boxes

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:18.240
the right way, that it unfairly puts the burden
on individuals when in reality that's not

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:19.660
where, where the burden is.

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:22.890
Can you talk a little bit about that, because
it seems, I mean from the book it seems as

00:07:22.890 --> 00:07:28.610
though you say that there needs to be structural
action, but individual action is also important.

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:29.610
Sure.

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:35.610
So, um, so in the book we, you know, we set
out what needs to happen in order to get us

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:39.090
on this trajectory to create a future that
we still can.

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:40.090
Right.

00:07:40.090 --> 00:07:42.990
And as you say, that's a variety of actions
that we can all participate in.

00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:45.290
Now there's three types of actions we talk
about.

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:48.970
One of those is actually who we are as human
beings.

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:54.020
Living through this fundamental transformation
is not easy for us to embrace that.

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:58.500
And we talk about how we can dig into some
of the deeper elements of the human psychology

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:02.420
to prepare ourselves for that and to be positive
actors for a great future.

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:06.140
The second thing we talk about is our own
activities.

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:11.560
So it is true that we are economic actors,
where political actors are, we're citizens,

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:16.340
we're consumers, we're parents, we're employees,
we're teachers, whatever we are.

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:20.670
And in all of those roles, there's a huge
amount that we can do to create a positive

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:21.670
future.

00:08:21.670 --> 00:08:22.670
And it's very meaningful, right?

00:08:22.670 --> 00:08:23.670
Everyday counts.

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:27.300
Now whether that changing your diet, whether
it's changing the way you move, change and

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:31.520
the way you heat your home, et cetera, or
re-imagining your role in the world so that

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:35.810
you can actually do something that has a more
positive impact or less of a negative impact.

00:08:35.810 --> 00:08:38.250
And that's really important right here.

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:42.969
Individuals have a major role in pushing systems
to actually reach tipping points.

00:08:42.969 --> 00:08:47.630
But we also say, and this is the third category
of action that we outlined in the book, you

00:08:47.630 --> 00:08:53.120
know, we need to use the power that we have
in relation to national governments in relation

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:58.900
to corporations, in relation to regional governments
and state governments to push them to participate

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:03.070
in civil disobedience, vote in a way that
is consistent with the kind of future we want

00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:04.560
to create this.

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:09.960
We've left this so late that we can no longer
say it's the responsibility of business or

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:12.360
investors or governments or individuals.

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:15.120
This has to be an everyone everywhere type
of initiative.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:20.120
And I think the problem with that dialogue
of where does responsibility lie is it can

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:21.190
be cyclical, right?

00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:24.870
You know, one time it's fashionable to say
it's individuals, another time it's fashionable

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:26.000
to say it's governments.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:29.390
The truth is it's everyone and it's urgent
and we need to be on this now.

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:34.490
Yeah, I mean it seems that in a way it's sure
it can be about recycling and plastic straws

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:37.590
and maybe looking at an electric car or something
like that.

00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:42.360
But at the same time, it's about the ability
of a population to at a certain point say,

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:46.380
unless you're seriously going to deal with
this, we don't vote you into office.

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:47.550
Right, exactly.

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:48.680
Or more than that.

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:52.000
You know, we go out on the streets and we
shut down operations until, you know, the

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:55.860
first duty of a democratic government is to
its population.

00:09:55.860 --> 00:10:03.110
By that metric, most governments in the West
are failing their most fundamental responsibility.

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:06.140
Some are failing reasonably badly, some are
failing really badly.

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:08.680
Um, and some are doing a reasonably good job.

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:13.550
We've been speaking with Tom rivet Karnak
who is co author of the book the future.

00:10:13.550 --> 00:10:16.650
We choose surviving the climate crisis.

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:19.029
Also the podcast outrage

00:10:19.029 --> 00:10:22.840
and optimism, which you should absolutely
check out.

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:24.230
Tom, thanks again for your time.

00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:26.300
I so appreciate it.

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:27.300
Thank you so much.

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:27.304
Great to be here.

